The '''Kanedans''' (金田人, ''Kanedajin'') are the citizens and primary ethnocultural population of [[Kaneda]]. Formed through centuries of migration, intermarriage, trade, and frontier settlement, the Kanedan people are regarded as one of the most ethnically and culturally diverse populations in the Sanese world. Unlike the comparatively homogeneous society of [[Nakamizu]], Kanedan national identity developed as a pluralistic civic culture shaped by indigenous traditions, maritime migration, and colonial-era settlement.

Kanedans primarily descend from settlers of [[Sanese people|Sanese]] origin, particularly migrants associated with the [[Kurokawa clan]] who established trading settlements in southern [[Lurandia]] during the late 15th century. Over subsequent centuries, these settlers mixed extensively with indigenous Kanedan peoples, Zong merchant communities, migrants from North Fosia, and subsequent [[Sea Wolves]] involvement. In recent years there are also influx of [[Illypnians]] immigrants from [[United Midlands]] among the working class. The resulting society evolved into a distinct multicultural identity centered on shared civic institutions rather than singular ethnic ancestry.

As of 2026, there are approximately 26 million Kanedans residing in Kaneda and an additional 4 million members of the Kanedan diaspora abroad. Kanedans constitute the overwhelming majority of the population of Kaneda, though many identify with multiple ancestral and cultural backgrounds. The country’s official multicultural policies, federal structure, and regional autonomy have contributed to the preservation of indigenous cultures and minority traditions alongside mainstream Kanedan society.

== Etymology ==

The term ''Kanedajin'' (金田人) literally means “people of Kaneda” or “people from the golden fields.” Historically, the term referred specifically to settlers and subjects of the Kurokawa-administered territories in southern Lurandia, though by the 19th century it came to encompass all citizens of the emerging Kanedan republic regardless of ethnic background.

In modern usage, “Kanedan” generally denotes a civic nationality rather than a singular ethnic lineage. This distinction is commonly contrasted with ethnic notions of identity in Nakamizu, where ancestry and regional heritage traditionally play a stronger role in national identity.

== Origins ==
=== Indigenous peoples ===

Prior to Sanese settlement, the islands and coastal territories of modern Kaneda were inhabited by numerous indigenous peoples collectively referred to as the [[Native Kanedans]]. These communities maintained maritime trading networks throughout southern [[Lurandia]] and developed complex fishing, woodworking, and ceremonial traditions adapted to the temperate coastal environment.

Many aspects of contemporary Kanedan culture, including canoe craftsmanship, coastal architecture, communal seafood traditions, and regional mythology, are heavily influenced by indigenous heritage. Indigenous place names remain widespread throughout the country, particularly in northern and western regions.

=== Sanese settlement ===

The first major wave of overseas settlement began in 1478, when the [[Kurokawa clan]] established a fortified trading port in southern Lurandia to control maritime commerce between the southern oceanic routes and mainland [[Nakamizu]]. Initially consisting primarily of merchants, sailors, and retainers from [[Yura Province]], the colony gradually expanded into surrounding territories.

Following the collapse of Kurokawa power in Ishiyama during the late 16th century, large numbers of refugees, soldiers, artisans, and merchants relocated permanently to Kaneda. These migrants formed the demographic and political foundation of the future Kanedan state.

=== Zong and North Fosian migration ===

From the 17th century onward, merchants and laborers from [[Zong]]-speaking regions settled in major Kanedan ports, particularly in [[Kanakou]] and southern coastal cities. Zong communities became influential in commerce, shipbuilding, fisheries, and finance, contributing significantly to urban culture and cuisine.

Migration from North Fosia increased during the industrial era, particularly during the 19th and early 20th centuries. Communities of North Fosian ancestry became concentrated in agricultural districts and industrial centers, contributing to Kaneda’s linguistic and culinary diversity.

== Ethnic composition ==

Kanedans are commonly described as a “multi-origin settler people” due to the country’s long history of integration between indigenous populations and overseas migrants. According to modern demographic estimates, the population includes people of:

* Sanese ancestry
* Indigenous Kanedan ancestry
* Zong ancestry
* North Fosian ancestry
* Illypnian ancestry
* Mixed or multi-ethnic backgrounds
* Smaller communities of western Fosian and southern maritime origin

Interethnic marriage rates in Kaneda are among the highest in the Sanese world. Mixed ancestry identities are widely normalized within Kanedan society, particularly in urban centers.
 
== Culture ==

Kanedan culture is characterized by strong regional diversity and syncretism. Cultural historians frequently describe Kaneda as a “Pacific frontier civilization,” combining Sanese institutional traditions with indigenous coastal customs and immigrant influences.

== Language ==

The primary language of Kaneda is [[Sanese language|Lurandian Sanese]], a distinct variety of Sanese shaped by centuries of contact with indigenous and immigrant languages. Regional dialects often incorporate vocabulary from native Kanedan and Zong languages.

Multilingualism is common in urban areas, particularly among immigrant communities and indigenous populations.

== Cuisine ==

Kanedan cuisine reflects the country’s maritime geography and multicultural history. Seafood forms a central component of national cuisine, alongside preserved fish, smoked meats, noodles, rice dishes, root vegetables, and North Fosian spices.

Fusion dishes combining Sanese and indigenous culinary traditions are especially associated with the Pacific coast. Zong-inspired street foods and North Fosian noodle dishes are also prominent in major cities.

== Arts and identity ==

Modern Kanedan identity emphasizes pluralism, coexistence, and regional autonomy. Indigenous carving traditions, maritime folk music, frontier literature, and contemporary multicultural arts are considered major components of the national culture.

Kaneda’s national mythology frequently portrays the country as a “nation of arrivals,” shaped less by bloodline than by participation in a shared civic and maritime society.

== Diaspora ==

Approximately 4 million people of Kanedan descent live outside Kaneda. Large diaspora communities exist in [[Nakamizu]], western Lurandia, and major port cities throughout Fosia.

Kanedan diaspora communities historically emerged through maritime commerce, industrial migration, and educational exchange. In many regions, Kanedans are associated with shipping, engineering, fisheries, and international trade industries.

